# termdock.conf — example configuration.
#
# Copy this to ~/.config/termdock/termdock.conf and edit, or skip it
# entirely: every setting below is optional and shown at its default, and
# termdock can write the file for you. Inside a session, Ctrl-B C opens
# the settings screen — pressing S there saves the setting you're editing
# to this file, as does ":set -p <key> <value>" from the command prompt
# (Ctrl-B :). Either one creates the file and its directory.
#
# A "#" starts a comment, on a line of its own or after a value. The one
# exception is a setting's first value word, so "status-bg #ff0000" still
# means the colour.
#
# Anything unrecognised — a key that doesn't exist, a value that doesn't
# parse — is ignored and the default kept, so a stale config from an
# older version never stops termdock starting.

# --- keys and input ---------------------------------------------------

# The prefix key, as a Ctrl+letter chord. Everything termdock does is
# behind it, so Ctrl-B ? for the full list.
prefix C-b

# Mouse support: click a pane to focus it, drag a border to resize, drag
# a window tab to reorder, click-drag on pane content to select and copy.
mouse on

# How long a bare arrow key keeps moving the focus after a prefixed one,
# in milliseconds — so crossing three panes is Ctrl-B ←←← rather than
# pressing the prefix each time. 0 turns it off.
repeat-time 1000

# Rebind one prefix command to a different key. Repeatable, one per line.
# Run Ctrl-B ? for the action names.
#bind M jump-picker
#bind Space respawn-pane

# --- panes ------------------------------------------------------------

# The shell new panes run. The default is your $SHELL, which is almost
# always what you want — set this only to run something other than your
# login shell. Read when a pane is created, so it affects the next one
# you open rather than the ones already running.
#shell /bin/bash

# Scrollback lines kept per pane, for copy-mode (Ctrl-B [) and search.
# Also read when a pane is created.
history-limit 10000

# What the floating popup (Ctrl-B P) runs instead of a shell.
#popup-command lazygit

# Send applications a terminal focus-in/focus-out event when you switch
# to or away from their pane — neovim's autoread, for one, acts on it.
focus-events off

# --- appearance -------------------------------------------------------

# A bundled colour preset. Run "termdock themes" for the full list:
# catppuccin, dracula, everforest, gruvbox, monokai, nord, one-dark,
# rose-pine, solarized, tokyo-night, ubuntu.
#theme dracula

# Individual colours, if you'd rather not use a theme (or want to change
# one of its choices). A theme sets all five; anything you set explicitly
# wins over it, whichever order they appear in. Values are a colour name
# or #rrggbb, and "default" means your terminal's own.
#status-bg black
#status-fg silver
#pane-active-bg teal
#pane-bg default
#pane-fg default

# Extra segments on the right of the status bar, comma separated:
# git (branch of the active pane's directory), battery, cpu, mem.
#status-segments git,battery
